\noindent This guide introduces students to tradable emission permits through an interactive simulation. You will learn the history, theory, and practical application of cap-and-trade systems---one of the most powerful market-based tools for environmental regulation.


\section{The Origins of Tradable Permits}

The concept of tradable pollution permits emerged from economic theory in the 1960s.\sidenote{Dales, J.H. (1968). \textit{Pollution, Property \& Prices}. University of Toronto Press.} John Dales first proposed the idea that pollution rights could be bought and sold like any other commodity, creating incentives for efficient pollution reduction.

\subsection{Why Tradable Permits?}

Traditional environmental regulation often uses \textit{command-and-control} approaches\sidenote{Command-and-control regulations specify exactly how much each polluter must reduce emissions, regardless of the cost.}---telling each polluter exactly how much to reduce. This is inefficient because:

\begin{itemize}
\item Different firms face different costs of reducing pollution
\item A firm that can reduce cheaply should reduce more
\item A firm facing high reduction costs should reduce less
\item Markets can find this efficient allocation automatically
\end{itemize}

\marginnote{The key insight: Let those who can reduce pollution cheaply do more of the reduction, while those facing high costs can buy permits instead.}

The tradable permit system harnesses market forces to achieve environmental goals at the lowest possible cost to society.

\section{Real-World Applications}

\subsection{The U.S. Acid Rain Program (1990)}

The most celebrated success story is the U.S. SO$_2$ trading program under the Clean Air Act Amendments of 1990.\sidenote{Schmalensee, R., \& Stavins, R.N. (2013). The SO$_2$ Allowance Trading System: The Ironic History of a Grand Policy Experiment. \textit{Journal of Economic Perspectives}, 27(1), 103-122.}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Goal}: Reduce sulfur dioxide emissions by 50\%
\item \textbf{Result}: Goal achieved two years early
\item \textbf{Cost}: One-third of initial estimates
\item \textbf{Innovation}: Spurred development of scrubber technology
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The European Union Emissions Trading System}

Launched in 2005, the EU ETS is the world's largest carbon market, covering over 11,000 installations.\sidenote{As of 2024, the EU ETS covers approximately 40\% of the EU's greenhouse gas emissions.}

\subsection{California's Cap-and-Trade Program}

California's program, started in 2013, covers about 80\% of the state's greenhouse gas emissions and has been linked with Quebec's system since 2014.

\section{How the Model Works}

Our simulation models a simplified cap-and-trade market with 24 firms. Understanding the model requires grasping four key concepts.

\subsection{The Emissions Cap}

The regulator sets a total emissions cap---the maximum amount of pollution allowed across all firms.\sidenote{In the simulation, this is the \textit{Emissions Cap} slider.} This cap is typically set below current emissions levels to force overall reductions.

\marginnote{The cap is the environmental guarantee: total emissions cannot exceed this level, regardless of economic conditions.}

\subsection{Initial Allocation}

Permits are initially distributed to firms. There are two main approaches:\sidenote{The simulation uses grandfathering, distributing permits equally among firms. In practice, allocation formulas are often based on historical emissions.}

\begin{description}
\item[Grandfathering] Free distribution based on past emissions
\item[Auctioning] Firms must purchase permits from the government
\end{description}

\subsection{Firm Heterogeneity}

Each firm has a unique \textit{cost level}\sidenote{In the simulation, you set this via the cost level inputs, ranging from 0.5 to 1.5.} representing the cost of reducing emissions:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Low-cost firms} (0.5--0.8): Can reduce emissions cheaply through efficiency improvements or fuel switching
\item \textbf{Medium-cost firms} (0.8--1.2): Face moderate abatement costs
\item \textbf{High-cost firms} (1.2--1.5): Expensive to reduce emissions; may need to buy permits
\end{itemize}

The cost level determines each firm's \textit{willingness to pay} for permits:

\begin{equation}
\text{Max WTP} = \text{Price Floor} + (\text{Price Ceiling} - \text{Price Floor}) \times \text{Cost Level}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Market Trading}

After initial allocation, firms compare their allocation to their desired emissions:\sidenote{Desired emissions reflect the firm's production needs and abatement opportunities.}

\begin{description}
\item[Surplus firms] Allocated more than needed $\rightarrow$ \textbf{Sellers}
\item[Deficit firms] Allocated less than needed $\rightarrow$ \textbf{Buyers}
\end{description}

\marginnote{A firm with surplus permits can profit by selling to deficit firms, while buyers avoid costly emission reductions.}

Trading continues until the market reaches equilibrium, where supply equals demand at a market price that clears the market.

\section{Understanding Market Dynamics}

\subsection{Supply and Demand Curves}

The simulation displays supply and demand curves:\sidenote{These curves are stylized representations. In reality, they would be step functions reflecting individual firm decisions.}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Supply curve}: Upward sloping, showing that higher prices induce more permit sales (firms reduce emissions more)
\item \textbf{Demand curve}: Downward sloping, showing that higher prices reduce permit demand (firms reduce emissions to avoid buying)
\item \textbf{Equilibrium}: Where curves intersect, determining market price and quantity traded
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Price Floor and Ceiling}

Real markets often include price constraints:\sidenote{California's cap-and-trade has both a price floor (auction reserve price) and a ceiling (allowance price containment reserve).}

\begin{description}
\item[Price Floor] Prevents permits from becoming too cheap, maintaining abatement incentives
\item[Price Ceiling] Prevents excessive costs that could harm the economy
\end{description}

\section{Using the Simulation}

\subsection{Basic Exploration}

\textbf{Step 1: Observe the baseline}
\begin{itemize}
\item Start with default parameters
\item Note the equilibrium price and quantity
\item Examine which firms are buyers vs. sellers
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 2: Adjust the cap}
\begin{itemize}
\item Reduce the emissions cap from 100 to 80 tons
\item What happens to the price?
\item Which firms can no longer afford to trade?
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 3: Explore firm heterogeneity}
\begin{itemize}
\item Set Firms 1--8 to cost level 1.5 (high cost)
\item Set Firms 9--16 to cost level 0.5 (low cost)
\item Set Firms 17--24 to cost level 1.0 (medium cost)
\item Observe trading patterns
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Advanced Scenarios}

\subsubsection{Scenario 1: Stringent Cap}

\textbf{Setup:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Emissions Cap: 70 tons
\item Total Demand: 140 tons
\item Price Floor: \$20
\item Price Ceiling: \$150
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Questions to explore:}
\begin{enumerate}
\item What is the shortage of permits?
\item How many firms cannot afford to trade?
\item What is the equilibrium price?
\item How does this compare to a less stringent cap?
\end{enumerate}

\marginnote{A stringent cap creates scarcity, driving up prices and forcing more emission reductions.}

\subsubsection{Scenario 2: Technology Shock}

Imagine a breakthrough in clean technology reduces costs for half the firms.

\textbf{Setup:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Set Firms 1--12 to cost level 0.6 (new technology)
\item Keep Firms 13--24 at their original levels
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Observe:}
\begin{itemize}
\item How does equilibrium price change?
\item Do low-cost firms become sellers?
\item What happens to market value?
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Scenario 3: Price Controls}

Test the impact of regulatory price constraints.

\textbf{Setup A: Binding floor}
\begin{itemize}
\item Set Price Floor to \$40 (high)
\item Observe which trades don't occur
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Setup B: Binding ceiling}
\begin{itemize}
\item Set Price Ceiling to \$60 (low)
\item Emissions Cap: 70 tons
\item Demand: 140 tons
\item What happens when demand exceeds supply at the ceiling price?
\end{itemize}

\section{Key Economic Concepts}

\subsection{Equimarginal Principle}

Tradable permits achieve the \textit{equimarginal principle}:\sidenote{At the optimum, the marginal cost of abatement should be equal across all firms.} all firms face the same marginal cost of reducing emissions (the permit price).

\begin{itemize}
\item Firms with low abatement costs reduce emissions and sell permits
\item Firms with high abatement costs buy permits instead of reducing
\item This achieves the cap at minimum total cost
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Static vs. Dynamic Efficiency}

\textbf{Static efficiency}: Achieving today's emissions target at lowest cost

\textbf{Dynamic efficiency}: Creating incentives for long-term innovation

\marginnote{Permit systems encourage firms to develop cleaner technologies. A firm that innovates can sell more permits or buy fewer.}

The simulation focuses on static efficiency, but real markets also drive innovation through the ongoing incentive to reduce costs.

\subsection{Market Power Concerns}

In real markets with few participants, firms might manipulate prices:\sidenote{The simulation assumes perfect competition, but real markets may have strategic behavior.}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Banking}: Saving permits for future use
\item \textbf{Hoarding}: Withholding permits to drive up prices
\item \textbf{Strategic allocation}: Gaming initial distribution
\end{itemize}

\section{Extensions and Limitations}

\subsection{What the Model Includes}

\begin{itemize}
\item Heterogeneous firms with different abatement costs
\item Initial allocation and trading
\item Price constraints
\item Market equilibrium
\end{itemize}

\subsection{What the Model Omits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Banking and borrowing}: Firms cannot save permits for future periods
\item \textbf{Uncertainty}: Firms have perfect information
\item \textbf{Transaction costs}: Trading is costless
\item \textbf{Monitoring and enforcement}: Compliance is assumed
\item \textbf{Offsets}: No credits from external reduction projects
\item \textbf{International linkages}: No connection to other markets
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion Questions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Why might a firm prefer grandfathered permits over auctioned permits? What are the equity implications?

\item If you were a regulator, how would you decide between a cap-and-trade system and a carbon tax?

\item What happens to the equilibrium price as the cap becomes more stringent over time? How would firms respond?

\item Why is it important that permits be tradable? What would happen if each firm simply had to meet its initial allocation?

\item How do price floors and ceilings affect market efficiency? When might they be desirable?

\item What role does initial allocation play in determining the final distribution of emissions? The final distribution of costs?

\item How might banking (saving permits for future use) affect firm behavior and market prices?

\item Consider environmental justice: Does a cap-and-trade system guarantee that pollution will be reduced in every location? Why or why not?
\end{enumerate}

\section{Further Reading}

\begin{itemize}
\item Stavins, R.N. (2008). Addressing climate change with a comprehensive US cap-and-trade system. \textit{Oxford Review of Economic Policy}, 24(2), 298-321.

\item Tietenberg, T. (2006). \textit{Emissions Trading: Principles and Practice} (2nd ed.). Resources for the Future.

\item Ellerman, A.D., Joskow, P.L., \& Harrison, D. (2003). \textit{Emissions Trading in the U.S.: Experience, Lessons, and Considerations for Greenhouse Gases}. Pew Center on Global Climate Change.

\item Hahn, R.W., \& Stavins, R.N. (2011). The Effect of Allowance Allocations on Cap-and-Trade System Performance. \textit{Journal of Law and Economics}, 54(4), S267-S294.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

Cap-and-trade systems represent a powerful application of economic theory to environmental policy. By creating a market for pollution rights, they harness private incentives to achieve public environmental goals efficiently.

The simulation allows you to explore how different firms with different costs interact in a permit market, how policy parameters affect outcomes, and why markets can achieve environmental goals more efficiently than command-and-control regulation.

\marginnote{Remember: The goal is not just to understand how the model works, but to develop intuition about how market-based environmental policies function in the real world.}

As you experiment with different scenarios, think about the policy trade-offs involved: stringency vs. cost, efficiency vs. equity, simplicity vs. precision. These are the challenges facing real-world environmental policymakers today.

